# Copy to .env for local overrides. .env itself is gitignored.

# Base path for built assets. Defaults to /WoW_VOD_Review/ (GitHub Pages project
# site). Set to / if you move to a custom domain, which serves from the root.
# VITE_BASE=/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Warcraft Logs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pull browser and death markers use the WCL v2 API. Users sign in with
# their own Warcraft Logs account via OAuth authorization code + PKCE.
#
# Register the client at https://www.warcraftlogs.com/api/clients/ and tick
# "Public Client (Only the PKCE code flow will be usable)". That is what makes
# it work with no secret — which matters because a secret CANNOT live in a
# static frontend: everything shipped to the browser is public, and GitHub
# Pages has no server to hide one behind.
#
# Set the redirect URLs to your Pages URL and your local dev URL.
#
# Signing in as the user is also what makes PRIVATE and GUILD reports work.

# VITE_WCL_CLIENT_ID=your_wcl_public_client_id

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Twitch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Needed to look up a channel's VODs by name. Register an app at
# https://dev.twitch.tv/console/apps with OAuth redirect URLs for both
# http://localhost:5173 (or whichever dev port you use) and your Pages URL.
#
# Only the Client ID is used, and it is public by design — it appears in the
# authorize URL every user is sent to. The app uses the *implicit grant* flow,
# which exists specifically for clients that cannot hold a secret, so the
# Twitch client SECRET is not needed here and must never be added.

# VITE_TWITCH_CLIENT_ID=your_twitch_client_id

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never put a client SECRET in this file or any VITE_ variable — everything
# prefixed VITE_ is inlined into the built JavaScript and served to the public.
# That applies to both Warcraft Logs and Twitch. If a flow needs a secret, it
# needs a backend; there is nowhere to hide one in a static site.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
